1
00:00:00,218 --> 00:00:02,694
Transcatheter ASD Device Closure,

2
00:00:02,825 --> 00:00:07,456
explained by Dr. Mohd Maqbool
Sohil, Interventional Cardiologist.

3
00:00:07,818 --> 00:00:12,315
Heart anatomy, front view:
oxygen-poor blood on the right side,

4
00:00:12,365 --> 00:00:16,496
in blue, and oxygen-rich blood
on the left side, in red.

5
00:00:16,804 --> 00:00:21,685
Secundum ASD: a deficiency of the
septum in the fossa ovalis region.

6
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:25,504
Blood shunts from the left
atrium to the right atrium.

7
00:00:25,891 --> 00:00:28,439
Defects are classified by location.

8
00:00:29,031 --> 00:00:33,981
Only the secundum type, in the fossa
ovalis, is suited to device closure;

9
00:00:34,031 --> 00:00:36,996
the other types are usually
repaired surgically.

10
00:00:37,493 --> 00:00:41,696
A left-to-right atrial shunt causes
right heart volume overload.

11
00:00:42,396 --> 00:00:48,527
A Qp:Qs above 1.5 indicates a
haemodynamically significant shunt.

12
00:00:48,851 --> 00:00:52,968
Closure is indicated for a
secundum ASD with right heart

13
00:00:53,018 --> 00:00:57,158
volume overload and acceptable
pulmonary vascular resistance,

14
00:00:57,208 --> 00:00:59,649
using typical guideline thresholds.

15
00:01:00,018 --> 00:01:03,779
Transoesophageal echo measures
the defect and its rims.

16
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:08,277
Rims of at least 5 mm
are generally adequate,

17
00:01:08,408 --> 00:01:11,991
and a deficient retro-aortic
rim is usually acceptable.

18
00:01:12,338 --> 00:01:13,278
Step one.

19
00:01:13,859 --> 00:01:16,561
Femoral venous access, with heparin.

20
00:01:17,204 --> 00:01:22,095
A catheter is advanced via the
IVC into the right atrium,

21
00:01:22,145 --> 00:01:26,728
under TEE/ICE and fluoroscopic guidance.

22
00:01:27,138 --> 00:01:27,947
Step two.

23
00:01:28,576 --> 00:01:31,264
The defect is crossed
into the left atrium,

24
00:01:31,314 --> 00:01:34,823
with a stiff wire usually parked
in a left pulmonary vein,

25
00:01:34,873 --> 00:01:36,826
and the delivery sheath follows.

26
00:01:37,238 --> 00:01:38,178
Step three.

27
00:01:38,795 --> 00:01:44,114
A compliant sizing balloon is inflated
across the defect until colour flow stops:

28
00:01:44,164 --> 00:01:45,676
the stop-flow diameter.

29
00:01:46,091 --> 00:01:50,124
The stop-flow diameter guides
device size: a self-expanding,

30
00:01:50,174 --> 00:01:54,460
double-disc nitinol occluder, whose
connecting waist fills the defect.

31
00:01:56,571 --> 00:01:57,476
Step four.

32
00:01:58,116 --> 00:02:02,304
The left atrial disc of the occluder
is opened in the left atrium,

33
00:02:02,354 --> 00:02:05,009
then pulled back flush against the septum.

34
00:02:05,404 --> 00:02:06,357
Step five.

35
00:02:06,914 --> 00:02:11,021
The waist fills the defect and
the right atrial disc is opened.

36
00:02:11,628 --> 00:02:14,533
The septal rims are held
between the two discs.

37
00:02:14,938 --> 00:02:15,890
Step six.

38
00:02:16,495 --> 00:02:21,945
The position is confirmed
by TEE/ICE and fluoroscopy:

39
00:02:21,995 --> 00:02:26,019
one disc on each side of the
septum, with the rims captured.

40
00:02:26,358 --> 00:02:29,915
Before release, a gentle
push-pull, or Minnesota,

41
00:02:29,965 --> 00:02:34,700
test with TEE/ICE and
fluoroscopy confirms that

42
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:38,489
the device is stable and
clear of nearby structures.

43
00:02:38,871 --> 00:02:39,835
Step seven.

44
00:02:40,416 --> 00:02:45,497
After a stability test, the delivery
cable is unscrewed to release the device,

45
00:02:45,547 --> 00:02:49,166
and the sheath is withdrawn down the IVC.

46
00:02:49,558 --> 00:02:51,070
The shunt is abolished.

47
00:02:51,674 --> 00:02:55,400
Over the following months, the
device is covered by tissue,

48
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,900
a process called endothelialisation.

49
00:02:58,284 --> 00:03:03,772
Complications are uncommon, but include
embolisation, erosion and arrhythmia.

50
00:03:04,341 --> 00:03:07,803
Antiplatelet therapy and
endocarditis prophylaxis

51
00:03:07,853 --> 00:03:10,449
typically continue for about six months.

52
00:03:10,838 --> 00:03:11,978
Patient selection,

53
00:03:12,028 --> 00:03:14,347
echo-guided sizing and a stable device

54
00:03:14,397 --> 00:03:17,909
position are central to
safe ASD device closure.

55
00:03:19,858 --> 00:03:25,036
This animation is for education and is
not a substitute for medical advice.

